hi there Steve Kaufman here first of all  if you like these videos about language  learning please subscribe to my channel  and also let me know if there's anything  you want me to talk about in particular  today I'm gonna talk about the  revolution in language learning of the  21st century before I do that I want to  say that I generally make my videos in  English I try to speak slowly and  clearly  so that people whose first language is  not English have a chance of  understanding me and then we make a  transcript of the video available at  link as a lesson where you can study the  text listen to the audio pick up any  words and phrases that you might have  missed and I think this way I reach the  broadest possible audience I am prepared  however to have conversations with any  of you who are native speakers of other  languages languages that I speak there's  about 15 of them  I might have to prepare for some of them  but I'm very happy to have conversations  on not just on language learning but  perhaps primarily on language learning  but the revolution in line was running  when I studied Chinese back in 1968 I  had a big open reel tape recorder that  sat in my room and if I wanted to listen  I had to listen they're sitting down  today I have my iPod Nano for when I am  exercising washing the dishes when I'm  very mobile this light blue iPod nano  nano has more audio in it than the  average language lab would have had back  50 years ago and it's all things of  interest to me on my iPhone I can take I  have the same audio as on my Nano but  this way if I'm sitting in the car  driving Bluetooth I can sync it to my  car speakers if there's a phone call I  can also pick up the phone call again  hands-free etcetera etc and I in the  meantime  and listen to my audio I can also  download audiobooks from audible.com and  have them all stored in here to listen  to I can also do link on my iPhone  although if I have the opportunity I  prefer to do it on my iPad so these  three tools are just phenomenal in terms  of making language learning more  efficient more comfortable easier now  fifty years ago my main activity in  order to you know improve my Chinese was  listening and reading I didn't know I  had never heard of Stephen crashing but  everything that stephen krashen says  about language acquisition through input  was exactly how I went about it I was  aware as a learner of Chinese I became  aware very early of the importance of  efficiency because other than your  attitude your motivation your confidence  the next most important factor is time  so if you can double the efficiency or  triple the efficiency of your  utilization of study time you know that  doesn't just double or triple your speed  of learning  I felt it geometrically increased my  speed of learning so very early I  decided I am NOT going to flip through a  dictionary because I forget the meaning  as soon as I closed the dictionary and  particularly with learning Chinese it  was very time consuming to look words up  in a dictionary  I looked for readers on a variety of  subjects which had glossaries the  inconvenience of the glossary obviously  is that the glossary can't anticipate  which words you know and don't know so  very often the words you want to find a  meaning for aren't there conversely  words that you know are in the glossary  list plus is distracting to flip back  and forth so the first major revolution  was the mp3 file initially the minidisc  mp3 fly files mp3 players the audio the  transport ability portability of audio  the second thing is the online  dictionary so you can be reading online  and immediately see the meaning  of course you can do things the way we  do at linked with these words and  phrases that you look up if it save them  you can review them you can do so many  things on a device like the iPhone or  the iPad that text comes alive and all  of that makes the learning much more  efficient granted it's more comfortable  to read a book but I find that until I  have a sufficient vocabulary level it's  frustrating to always come across words  that I don't know and of course look  them up in a dictionary forget the  meaning write out a bunch of lists  longhand that I never look at so the  availability of online dictionaries has  made it possible to access texts that  are perhaps too difficult but because  they are interesting they're ideal  learning material so again I want to  give credit and the third thing I want  to give credit to stephen krashen I  totally agree with what he says about  the importance of input eventually you  have to speak and when you speak you're  you're involved with the language you're  fighting you're trying to express  yourself it become very real the  resonance is very high but before you  get into the ring and to wrestle or to  box with the language you have to have  some weapons you have to be able to  understand what the other person is  saying you have to have some words to  defend yourself with and that's where  the importance of input based learning  comes in and I tell you when I wrote my  book about language learning this was  the book and we also translated into  Chinese was published in China I mean  all the things that I said there were  pure crashing before I heard of crashing  but crashing explains it so elegantly  he explains and he backs it up with  research and so if we couple what we now  know about the importance of input based  learning with the tools that are now  available to make language learning so  much more efficient and so much more  convenient that net effect plus the  availability of the internet and all the  resources that are there the net effect  is a  pollution a revolution not only in terms  of input based learning based learning  but eventually even in terms of talking  to people and communicating with people  it's a wonderful world the 21st century  and I should say that we often hear a  lot of negativity about the world today  I am such an optimist I feel it's a  wonderful time to be alive yes there are  problems in the Middle East problems in  in Ukraine problems of in Africa and  wherever there are always problems there  are wheels will be problems but there  always were problems but in terms of the  improvement of living standards for a  vast majority of people in the world the  number of people have been taken out of  poverty what we're now seeing in terms  of the reducing price or cost of solar  and wind power the ability of humans to  come up with technical solutions for the  problems that we have not the least of  which is technical improvements in the  learning of languages I am an optimist  and I think to be a language learner I  think it helps to be an optimist because  you have to believe that you can do it  you have to believe that it's worthwhile  you have to believe that the method  you're using is gonna take you to where  you want to get to in terms of achieving  fluency so a bit of a ramble today but I  do believe the 21st century is  revolutionary when it comes to language  learning we should be aware of it  we should take advantage of it kids in  school shouldn't just learn one language  they should learn two languages and  discover more of the world and it's now  easier to do than it ever was so thanks  for listening  a bit of a long ramble bye for now  you
 

